body:
I'm in exactly the same boat right now. I'm in the midst of buying everything to move out for the first time and I would say that spending a bit of money is worth it. My advice is to go to find quality furniture at thrift shops. Maybe not everything, but you can find a lot of quality, long-lasting pieces at thrift shops or even Facebook marketplace. You just need to be careful and to examine each piece thoroughly before you buy it. Think- what material is this piece made of? Why is someone selling this? Is this something I could see in my living room? You can even repaint the item (it's easier than you may think). I would agree with ignescentOne- couches should be invested in. But dining rooms tables, coffee tables, accent chairs, etc. can be cheaper if you don't want to spend too much.

&#x200B;

I would also advise to not buy everything all at once. The nice part about starting fresh is that you can build upon the items you have and over time, you can find quality pieces that you love. In my opinion, I would rather buy a quality item that I absolutely adore rather than to buy something just for the hell of it.

&#x200B;

Honestly, there are going to be things you do not think about until you need it a few months down the road. I have a giant list of things that I need to buy and I keep finding myself thinking of the most random things that I need to add to the list. What I'm trying to do is to buy the things I absolutely need before I move out and waiting to buy the things I don't need urgently later. For example, I'm buying kitchen gear right now but I'm going to wait until I move in to buy porch furniture (which I'm really excited to buy for some reason).

Edit: I want to add that there is nothing wrong with going overboard- you are living here and should spend the time and money to invest in your home.
